56. Most Important Architecture Rule

Do NOT implement this as:

Chaos Monkey
      |
      +--> Restart Postgres
      +--> Restart Kafka
      +--> Restart IBM MQ

Instead implement:

                         CHAOS FRAMEWORK
                               |
             +-----------------+------------------+
             |                 |                  |
             v                 v                  v
       Code Injection      Pod Control       Verification
             |
      +------+-------+---------+---------+
      |              |         |         |
      v              v         v         v
   PostgreSQL      IBM MQ    Kafka     Storage
   boundary       boundary  boundary   boundary
      |              |         |         |
      v              v         v         v
    External       External  External  External
    PostgreSQL     IBM MQ    Kafka     NFS/S3

The framework controls how the application experiences failures, while external teams continue to own the infrastructure.

The final system should make it possible for an operator to select:

“Kill one Prefund pod in DC1 immediately after DB commit, then verify outbox recovery and ensure no duplicate MQ reservation.”

or:

“For the next five settlement transactions, inject a 10-second IBM MQ response timeout and verify retry, idempotency, reconciliation, and final settlement completion.”

or:

“Inject PostgreSQL timeout during settlement matching, restart one application pod, then verify that the settlement is eventually processed exactly once.”

The simulator should turn each of these into a repeatable, auditable experiment with measurable input → fault → system behavior → recovery → business outcome.

This prompt is intentionally structured so the Chaos capability remains separate from the ACH business logic, while still having injection points at every critical processing boundary. That separation will make it much easier to enable/disable the capability, test it independently, and eventually deploy it as a reusable internal framework across other .NET payment services.
